{"id":"ltc-related-balance-check","title":"The \"$1.14 billion owed by related companies\", checked against the complete data sets","question":"Does the State's full report data confirm the amount Bay Area nursing homes say related companies owe them, and which homes hold it?","finding":"Re-measured from the State's complete data sets, 42 of 183 Bay Area nursing homes carry half or more of their assets, and at least $3 million, as money owed by related parties at the end of their latest report: $1,021.5 million. The case figure of $1,021.5 million for 42 homes is confirmed. 3 of the homes are in San Francisco ($63.4 million: San Francisco Post Acute, Victorian Post Acute, Central Gardens Post Acute). The 42 homes reported paying related companies $72.3 million in 2025.","how":"Each home's report in the State's complete long-term care data set (report years 2019 (audited), 2022 (audited), 2023 (as submitted), 2024 (as submitted), 2025 (as submitted)) was read cell by cell: page 5.1 lines 50 and 160 (receivables from related parties, current and non-current), page 5.2 lines 45 and 110 (payables to related parties), page 5.1 line 200 (total assets). Same cut as the existing screen: Bay Area skilled-nursing licences with a report in the latest two years; at least half of assets and at least $3 million; a home owing and owed more than five times a year's revenue at once is set aside. Where a home filed two reports in a year (a change of operator), this screen uses the report that ends last. \"Paid to related parties\" is the sum of the transaction amounts the home itself listed on page 10.4 (lines 30 to 99) of the same reports.","limits":"A balance owed by a related company is not a payment and not a loss: chains commonly sweep each home's cash to a central office and book what the home is owed. When an operator changes, the outgoing operator's receivable from its own affiliates normally leaves with it; the State file does not say whether it was repaid. Reports for 2023 